---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ModuleGraph from "../../utils/distributed/graph/ModuleGraph.astro";
import ffn from "../../utils/distributed/graph/ffnRankExample.json";
import megatronMoe from "../../utils/distributed/graph/megatronMoe.json";
import type {
	ModuleSpec,
	WeightSpec,
} from "../../utils/distributed/graph/moduleGraph";

// The FFN section uses one FFN (8 → 32 → 8, ReLU, no biases) before and after a torch run: the
// teacher y = A x has rank 2, and AdamW with weight decay 0.1 drives both weights to about two
// strong directions. Its weights are the run's, rounded to 4 decimals; the component computes
// the histograms, singular values and ranks from them.

// A residual MLP block, written by hand: a submodule, leaf Linear modules, and a tensor (x) that
// enters the submodule and is also read outside it. Shapes and weights, no tensor values.
const t = (name: string, shape: number[], symbolic_shape: string) => ({
	name,
	shape,
	symbolic_shape,
});
// Weights from a seeded generator: fc1 as at initialization (Gaussian, std 1/√in, full rank), fc2
// collapsed to rank 2 plus small noise, which the hover card's ranks and spectrum show.
let seed = 7;
const uniform = () => {
	seed = (seed + 0x6d2b79f5) | 0;
	let t = Math.imul(seed ^ (seed >>> 15), 1 | seed);
	t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t;
	return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 2 ** 32;
};
const gauss = () =>
	Math.sqrt(-2 * Math.log(1 - uniform())) * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * uniform());
const matrix = (m: number, n: number, f: (i: number, j: number) => number) =>
	Array.from({ length: m }, (_, i) =>
		Array.from({ length: n }, (_, j) => Number(f(i, j).toFixed(4))),
	);
const fc1Weight: WeightSpec = {
	shape: [64, 16],
	values: matrix(64, 16, () => gauss() / 4),
	note: "As initialized: std 1/√16.",
};
const [u, v] = [matrix(16, 2, gauss), matrix(2, 64, gauss)];
const fc2Weight: WeightSpec = {
	shape: [16, 64],
	values: matrix(
		16,
		64,
		(i, j) => (u[i][0] * v[0][j] + u[i][1] * v[1][j]) / 8 + 0.002 * gauss(),
	),
	rank_tol: 0.05,
	note: "Collapsed: rank 2 plus noise of std 0.002. rank_tol = 0.05 counts only σ above 5% of σ₁.",
};

const block: ModuleSpec = {
	name: "",
	type: "module",
	class: "ResidualBlock",
	symbols: {
		tokens: "rows of the batch: batch size · sequence length",
		hidden: "the model width; 16 here",
	},
	inputs: { x: t("x", [8, 16], "(tokens, hidden)") },
	operations: {
		add: {
			type: "op",
			op: "torch.add",
			label: "add (residual)",
			inputs: { input: "x", other: "mlp.out" },
			outputs: { out: t("y", [8, 16], "(tokens, hidden)") },
			equation: {
				title: "Residual",
				latex: ["y = x + \\operatorname{MLP}(\\operatorname{LN}(x))"],
			},
		},
		norm: {
			type: "module",
			class: "LayerNorm",
			inputs: { input: "x" },
			outputs: { out: t("normed", [8, 16], "(tokens, hidden)") },
		},
		mlp: {
			type: "module",
			class: "MLP",
			inputs: { h: "norm.out" },
			operations: {
				fc1: {
					type: "module",
					class: "Linear",
					inputs: { input: "h" },
					outputs: { out: t("fc1(h)", [8, 64], "(tokens, 4·hidden)") },
					weights: { weight: fc1Weight },
				},
				gelu: {
					type: "op",
					op: "F.gelu",
					inputs: { input: "fc1.out" },
					outputs: { out: t("gelu", [8, 64], "(tokens, 4·hidden)") },
				},
				fc2: {
					type: "module",
					class: "Linear",
					inputs: { input: "gelu.out" },
					outputs: { out: t("fc2", [8, 16], "(tokens, hidden)") },
					weights: { weight: fc2Weight },
				},
			},
			outputs: { out: "fc2.out" },
			// A module's weights are its named_parameters().
			weights: { "fc1.weight": fc1Weight, "fc2.weight": fc2Weight },
		},
	},
	outputs: { y: "add.out" },
};
---

<Layout title="ModuleGraph demo">
	<h1>ModuleGraph demo</h1>
	<p>
		<code>ModuleGraph</code> draws a PyTorch module from nested JSON (<code>utils/distributed/graph/moduleGraph.ts</code>): ops and
		leaf modules as nodes, modules as nested boxes, tensors as edges. Hover over an edge for its tensor, a node for its equation,
		shapes and weights, and a box's dotted label for its weights. Click any of them to keep its card open; click again,
		click elsewhere or press Escape to close it. The names in shapes are explained from the spec's
		<code>symbols</code>.
	</p>

	<h2>A residual MLP block (shapes and weights)</h2>
	<p>
		<code>fc1</code> and <code>fc2</code> list weights: hover <code>fc2</code> to see a collapsed one (rank 2 of 16), or
		<code>mlp: MLP</code> for both.
	</p>
	<ModuleGraph spec={block} title="ResidualBlock.forward" />

	<h2>An FFN's weights before and after training</h2>
	<p>
		A two-layer feed-forward network, <code>fc2(relu(fc1(x)))</code> with widths 8 → 32 → 8 and no biases, trained on
		<code>y = A x</code> where <code>A</code> has rank 2 (512 random inputs, AdamW, lr 0.01, weight decay 0.1, 3000 steps,
		final MSE 1.4 × 10⁻⁶). Hover over <code>fc1</code> or <code>fc2</code> for one weight, or over the dotted
		<code>FFN</code> label for both. The weights come from a torch run, rounded to 4 decimals.
	</p>

	<h3>At initialization</h3>
	<p>
		PyTorch's default uniform initialization: the histogram is flat, and the singular values fall slowly. The effective
		rank is close to the maximum of 8, so every direction is in use.
	</p>
	<ModuleGraph spec={ffn.init as ModuleSpec} title="FFN at initialization" />

	<h3>After training</h3>
	<p>
		The map to learn needs only two directions, and weight decay shrinks the rest. Two singular values stay large and a
		third is about 14% of σ₁ in <code>fc1</code>, 23% in <code>fc2</code> (the ReLU needs a little extra to represent a
		linear map); the rest fall below 8%. The effective rank drops from about 7.7 to about 3. The numerical rank still
		reads 8 of 8, because no singular value is exactly zero. Rank collapse shows in the effective and stable ranks
		and the spectrum, not in the exact rank.
	</p>
	<ModuleGraph spec={ffn.trained as ModuleSpec} title="FFN after training" />

	<h2>A Megatron-style MoE layer with expert parallelism</h2>
	<p>One EP rank's forward pass, collectives in orange; values are rank 0's from an EP = 2 run.</p>
	<ModuleGraph spec={megatronMoe as ModuleSpec} wide />
</Layout>
